     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "when et while",
     "md": "- **while** + past continuous : While I **was walking**, I met Amina.\n- **when** + past simple (action courte) : I was walking **when** it **started** to rain.\n- Deux actions longues en même temps : While Boris **was cooking**, Amina **was washing** the plates."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Exemple 1 : action interrompue",
     "statement": "Relie les deux phrases avec when : I (watch) TV. The lights (go) out.",
     "steps": [
      {
       "md": "L'action longue est au past continuous : I was watching TV."
      },
      {
       "md": "L'action courte est au past simple : the lights went out."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "**I was watching TV when the lights went out.**"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Exemple 2 : negation",
     "statement": "Écris à la forme négative : Mballa played football yesterday.",
     "steps": [
      {
       "md": "Au past simple, la négation utilise didn't + verbe de base (sans -ed)."
      },
      {
       "md": "On relit la phrase obtenue pour vérifier la forme du verbe et le sens."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "**Mballa didn't play football yesterday.**"
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Erreurs fréquentes",
     "md": "- « Did you went ? » : après did, on revient au verbe de base : Did you **go** ?\n- « I was go » : après was/were il faut -ing : I was **going**.\n- Employer le past continuous pour une action courte et terminée : on dit « I met Amina yesterday », pas « I was meeting Amina yesterday »."
    }
